# Notes on Click callback relationships

After rechecking `src/click/types.py`, the earlier grouping of these methods into a single "type conversion callback" path was too broad. What can be located directly in the file is `class ParamType:`, which brings together type-object display, the call entry point, and the conversion interface; adjacent names don't mean the same caller triggers them in sequence.

```python
def to_info_dict(self):
def __call__(self, value, param=None, ctx=None):
def get_metavar(self, param):
def get_missing_message(self, param):
def convert(self, value, param, ctx):
def split_envvar_value(self, rv):
```

Here, "callback relationships" is limited to the `__call__` layer: an instance can be called with `value`, `param`, and `ctx`. The signature of `convert` also accepts a parameter and context, so keep it distinct from `__call__` when reading specific types; the former is the explicitly named conversion interface, while the latter is the callable entry point.

`to_info_dict`, `get_metavar`, and `get_missing_message` are more closely related to descriptive information and missing-parameter messages. `split_envvar_value` only accepts `rv`; this file alone doesn't establish it as a general parameter callback path.

Not yet checked: which subclasses override `__call__`, and who receives the environment-variable split results afterward. DOC-02 contains manual reproduction records; those aren't merged here.

# Help Width and Default Value Migration Notes

The earliest records treated `help width and defaults` and `short help and formatter width` as the same type of display issue. The troubleshooting entry point was `src/click/core.py`; first drill down through the terms `parameter`, `help`, `width`, and `defaults`, then note the nearby occurrences of `value`, `default`, and `formatter`.

The practice at the time was to save a help output, then use a different terminal width to inspect the line wrapping. The old notes tended to handle changes to the default-value text and formatter width together: when the output changed, go back to the same code section and look for `default`. This was convenient, but it did not distinguish where the displayed value came from or distinguish the summary itself from its line-wrapped appearance.

When migrating old examples, keep the original input and output; do not just take the first line. When tracing back through the old entry point, record things in the following order:

1. Parameter input and its help text;
2. The terminal's `size` or `columns` clues;
3. Local references near the formatter;
4. The complete paragraph at the beginning of the help text.

If the new example does not produce output that can be compared, first return to the old entry point for `src/click/core.py`; do not use text with lines manually removed in place of the execution result. There was no conclusion at the time on whether the default value and the short description had separate sources.
